      "body": "# What does this PR do?\n\nMakes the unsloth gradient-checkpointing custom autograd function and the reward-model device fallback **device-agnostic**, so LLaMA-Factory works on non-CUDA accelerators (Ascend NPU, Intel XPU, MPS, etc.).\n\n## Problem\n\n### 1. `get_unsloth_gradient_checkpointing_func()` — crash on NPU\nWhen `use_unsloth_gc=True`, the custom function uses CUDA-specific APIs:\n\n```python\n@torch.cuda.amp.custom_fwd\ndef forward(...):\n    ...\n@torch.cuda.amp.custom_bwd\ndef backward(...):\n    hidden_states = hidden_states.to(\"cuda\", non_blocking=True).detach()\n```\n\nOn an Ascend NPU environment (torch_npu, where `torch.cuda` is **not** compiled in), `hidden_states.to(\"cuda\")` raises:\n```\nRuntimeError: Torch not compiled with CUDA enabled\n```\nSo gradient checkpointing is broken on NPU.\n\n### 2. `hf_engine.py` device fallback hardcodes `\"cuda\"`\n```python\ndevice = getattr(model.pretrained_model, \"device\", \"cuda\")\n```\nOn NPU `torch.cuda.is_available()` is `False`, so a missing device attribute would incorrectly fall back to CUDA.\n\n## Fix\n\n- Use `torch.amp.custom_fwd/bwd(device_type=...)` with the device type resolved from `torch.accelerator.current_accelerator()` (falls back to `\"cuda\"` for torch < 2.4 / no accelerator module).\n- Save the compute device as `ctx._device` in `forward`, and move the saved `hidden_states` back to it in `backward` instead of hardcoding `\"cuda\"`.\n- Change the `hf_engine.py` fallback default to `torch.device(\"cpu\")`.\n\n## Verification\n\nVerified on **Ascend NPU (torch 2.14, torch_npu, 910B)**:\n- Full forward+backward through `UnslothGradientCheckpointing` runs correctly (`hidden_states` round-trips to NPU, gradients propagate).\n- The old `.to(\"cuda\")` pattern reproduces `RuntimeError: Torch not compiled with CUDA enabled` on the same setup, confirming the fix addresses a real NPU break.\n- `torch.amp.custom_fwd(device_type=\"npu\")` is confirmed valid on this environment.\n\nDevice-agnostic: on CUDA, `torch.accelerator.current_accelerator()` returns `\"cuda\"`, preserving existing behavior.\n",
